1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Andrzej z archdyakona kaliskiego. Biskup poznanski
2 CONT
2 CONT Copied from Wikipedia (uncorrected):
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrzej Zaremba from Czermina of the Zaremba coat of arms (died 1317 or 1318) was bishop of Poznań.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Szymon, castellan of Gniezno, brother of the cousin of Kalisz, Mikołaj Jankowic and Sędziwój of Jarocin. A representative of one of the most powerful families of Wielkopolska in the 13th and 14th centuries. No mention has b
2 CONC een made of his childhood and studies. For the first time he appears in the sources as a chaplain of Bolesław the Pious.[2] In 1280 he became the chancellor of Przemyśl II, and in 1282 he was also Archdiocese of Kalisz.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of the king, he works in the office of Władysław Łokietek. In a document from 1297, he appears as a provost of the Poznań chapter.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Episcopal career
2 CONT
2 CONT In the same year, he left the court of Łokietek to embrace the Poznań bishop's throne.[3] In 1298 he was the main negotiator of the settlement in Kościan, between Łokietek, Henry III Głogowczyk and wielkopolskie Wielkieks, who in 
2 CONC exchange for the support of Łokietek's embrace of the Crown left by Przemysl guaranteed a number of privileges to Poznań bishops, including the title of the Crown Chancellor and the Poznań throne.[2] Soon after, along with the maj
2 CONC ority of the magnates of Wielkopolska, he found himself in opposition to Łokietek. In 1299, Władysław Łokietek placed the interdict in the diocese of Poznań (by some historians, this fact is questioned). After that he became one o
2 CONC f the main architects of Wenceslas II's coronation as the king of Poland. After the death of Wacław II, and soon after him Wacław III supported in the summer of 1306 Henryk Głogowczyk, who mastered Greater Poland. The support of t
2 CONC he Silesian Piasts of German and Silesian influences in Wielkopolska quickly caused that the majority of the nobles stood in opposition to Henry and his sons. Jakub Świnka and Andrzej Zaremba were in the forefront of this movement
2 CONC . In 1312, both priests laid a curse on Henryk and his sons, which accelerated the end of their rule over Wielkopolska. In 1313 bp. Andrzej ultimately spoke on the side of Łokietek.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the office of the Poznań Bishop, he reformed the archdiocese of Poznań, dividing it into three smaller ones and planning to create a fourth one in the lands located on the northern bank of the Noteć. He issued laws that wou
2 CONC ld put an end to the distribution of tithing from both new parishes and help to regain those assignments that his predecessors left behind. In the episcopal administration, he created a permanent general office, settled matters of
2 CONC  cathedral vicars, created their permanent college, and recognized the bishops' chancellors as prelates. He also issued, in 1302, permission for an uprising at a Poznań friend.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT edit
2 CONT 1.  Cheney, David M. "Bishop Andrzej Zaremba [Catholic-Hierarchy]". www.catholic-hierarchy.org. Retrieved 17 February 2018.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  Antoni Gąsiorowski, Jerzy Topolski [ed.]: Wielkopolski Słownik Biograficzny. Warszawa-Poznań: Państwowe Wydawnictwo Naukowe, 1981, p. 24.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  Hierarchia Catholica, Volume 1, Page 407
