1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elizabeth of Pilica (also known by her married name, Elizabeth Granowska) was the Queen of Poland and Grand Duchess of Lithuania from 1417 until her death in 1420. She is highly distinctive in Polish history becaus
2 CONC e she was the only wife of King Władysław II Jagiełło who did not come from a ruling royal dynasty, but was instead a member of the Polish nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Early Life and Scandalous Background==
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth was born around 1372 as the only child of '''Otto of Pilica''', a wealthy voivode (governor) of Sandomierz. Inheriting vast estates, she became one of the wealthiest heiresses in Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her personal life before marrying the King was highly unusual and surrounded by controversy. Traditional historical records and contemporary rumors noted multiple marriages: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Wiseł Czambor:''' A Silesian knight who allegedly kidnapped her to secure her wealth.
2 CONT * '''Jan of Jičín''': Another knight who killed Czambor to marry Elizabeth himself.
2 CONT * '''Wincenty Granowski:''' A lower-nobility aristocrat whom she married around 1397. They had several children together before he died, possibly by poisoning, in 1410.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Royal Marriage and Backlash==
2 CONT
2 CONT  In March 1416, King Jagiełło's second wife, Anna of Cilli, died. By 1417, the King shocked the Polish court by announcing his intention to marry Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT The match was met with fierce opposition from the Polish nobility and royal councilors. Elizabeth was a middle-aged widow (around 45 at the time), deemed past childbearing age, lacking foreign political alliances, and carrying a s
2 CONC candalous reputation. Many believed the King married her out of genuine personal affection rather than political strategy. Despite the intense backlash, Jagiełło pushed forward, and they married on May 2, 1417; she was crowned Que
2 CONC en at Wawel Cathedral later that year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Death and Legacy==
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth's reign was short and marked by constant political isolation. She often traveled with the King but held minimal political influence. Just three years after her coronation, she died of tuberculosis on May 12, 1420, in Kra
2 CONC ków. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was initially buried in Wawel Cathedral. However, during later reburials and structural changes to the cathedral, her remains were moved, and the exact location of her grave remains unknown to this day.
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_Granowska Wikipedia: Elizabeth Granowska]
