1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jan_III_Sobieski Jan III Sobieski w Wikipedii po Polsku]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_III_Sobieski Jan III Sobieski on Wikipedia in English]
2 CONT
2 CONT John III Sobieski (Polish: Jan III Sobieski) (17 August 1629 – 17 June 1696) was one of the most notable monarchs of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, from 1674 until his death King of Poland and Grand Duke of Lithuania. Sobiesk
2 CONC i's 22-year-reign was marked by a period of the Commonwealth's stabilisation, much needed after the turmoil of the Deluge and Khmelnytsky Uprising. Popular among his subjects, he was also a military genius, most famous for the vic
2 CONC tory over the Turks in the 1683 Battle of Vienna. Following his victories over the Ottoman Empire, he was called by the Turks the "Lion of Lechistan" and held as the saviour of European Christendom by the pope.[
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Biography'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in 1629 at Olesko, Poland to Jakub (James) Sobieski (1580-1646), Voivod of Ruthenian Voivodship and Castellan of Kraków, Zofia Teofillia (Danilowicz).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1668 King Jan II Kazimierz appointed John Sobieski the Commander-in-Chief of the Polish army. After a distinguished military career, and following the death of King Jan II Kazimierz's successor, Michael Korybut Wisniowiecki, Jo
2 CONC hn Sobieski was elected by the szlachta as king of Poland on May 21, 1674 and was crowned on February 2, 1676.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Jan III Sobieski at the Battle of Vienna", painted by Jerzy Eleuter Szymonowicz-Siemiginowski, abt. 1686, National Museum in Warsaw.John Sobieski's military prowess, as exhibited in a war against the Ottoman Empire, contributed t
2 CONC o his election as king of Poland. Later he allied with the Holy Roman Emperor. His greatest success came on September 12, 1683 as victor at the Battle of Vienna, with Polish, Austrian and German troops, once more against the Turks
2 CONC  under Kara Mustafa. The pope and other foreign dignitaries then hailed Sobieski as the "Savior of Vienna and Western European civilization." In a letter to his wife he wrote, ...All the common people kissed my hands, my feet, my 
2 CONC clothes; others only touched me, saying: Ach, let us kiss so valiant a hand!".
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Oscar Halecki, noted Polish historical writer, John III planned to occupy Prussia with Swedish cooperation and French support. This undertaking was doomed to failure, because of the war with Turkey, the skillful diplo
2 CONC macy of the Elector of Brandenburg, and the frequent shifts of alliances amongst the western powers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon reaching Vienna, he joined up with the Austrians and Germans. Sobieski planned to attack on the 13th of September, but he had noticed that the Turkish resistance was weak and ordered full attack on September 12, 1683. At 4 a.
2 CONC m. in the morning Sobieski’s army of about 81,000 men attacked a Turkish army that numbered about 130,000. Sobieski charged with his hussars forward and soon after the Turkish battle line was broken as the Turks scattered in confu
2 CONC sion. At 5:30 p.m., Sobieski entered the deserted tent of Kara Mustafa and the battle of Vienna was over.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sobieski Sending Message of Victory to the Pope, after the Battle of Vienna", oil on canvas by Jan Matejko, 1880, 58 x 100 cm, National Musemum in Kraków.In a strange twist of events a statue of John III Sobieski was brought to t
2 CONC he city of Gdansk by people from his native land (from Lwów), when they were resettled there. Already John's family had been famous guests in the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sobieski with family painted by Henri Gascar.Now the statue overlooks the little park at the old Gdansk town hall, now a museum.
2 CONT
2 CONT King John III Sobieski, the last great king of Poland, died in Wilanów, Poland on June 17, 1696. His wife, Marie Casimire, died in 1716 in Blois, France and her body was returned to Poland. They are interred together in Wawel Cast
2 CONC le, Kraków, Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Notes'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT John III Sobieski (Polish: Jan III Sobieski, Lithuanian: Jonas Sobieskis; 17 August 1629 – 17 June 1696), from 1674 until his death King of Poland and Grand Duke of Lithuania, was one of the most notable monarchs of the Polish–Lit
2 CONC huanian Commonwealth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sobieski's 22-year reign was marked by a period of the Commonwealth's stabilization, much needed after the turmoil of the Deluge and Khmelnytsky Uprising.[1] Popular among his subjects, he was an able military commander, most famo
2 CONC us for the victory over the Turks in the 1683 Battle of Vienna.[2] Following his victories over the Ottoman Empire, the Ottomans named him the "Lion of Lechistan" and he was held as the saviour of European Christendom by the pope.
