1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Algirdas'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal House: Gediminids
2 CONT Grand Duke of Lithuania (Predecessor - Jaunutis, Successor - Jogaila)
2 CONT Born: ca 1296.
2 CONT Died: end of May 1377 in Maišiagala (?)
2 CONT Reign: 1316 - 1377, 1345 - 1377 as Grand Duke of Lithuania
2 CONT Consort: 1. Maria of Vitebsk; 2. Juliana / Uliana of Tver
2 CONT
2 CONT Allegedly baptized as Dmitry (according to the monument record in the Kyiv-Pechersk Lavra), late in life accepted the monastic name Alexius (or Aleksander). 
2 CONT
2 CONT GDL Algirdas's father is Gediminas (Giedymin). Mother is presumed: Olga of Smolensk. (All three GDL Gediminas' wives are presumed: Jaunė / Jewna, Vindemunda, and Olga; so either could be Algirdas's mother. 
2 CONT
2 CONT GDL Algirdas was married twice. First wife Maria Yaroslavna - the only daughter of the last Vitebsk prince Yaroslav Vasilyevich. It is assumed that around 1317 she was married to GDL Algirdas. She was the only heiress of the Viteb
2 CONC sk principality. After the death of her father around 1345, the principality was ceded to Algirdas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria gave birth to four or five sons and at least one daughter. There is an assumption, that the children of Maria and Algirdas grew up in Orthodox Russian lands and were themselves baptized according to the Orthodox rite. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Maria in 1346 (?), Algirdas married Juliana / Uliana, daughter of the Grand Duke of Tver Alexander Mikhailovich. With his second wife, Algirdas had seven or eight sons and several daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Algirdas was one of the seven sons of the Grand Duke Gediminas. Before his death in 1341 Gediminas divided his domains, leaving the youngest son Jaunutis in possession of the capital Vilnius, with a nominal priority. With the aid 
2 CONC of his brother Kęstutis, Algirdas drove out the incapable Jaunutis and declared himself a Grand Prince in 1345. Thirty-two years of his reign (1345-1377) were devoted to the development and expansion of the Grand Duchy of Lithuani
2 CONC a. Algirdas has managed to make it one of the greatest states in Europe and the largest in the continent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two factors are supposed to have contributed to achieving this result; the extraordinary political sagacity of Algirdas and the life-long devotion of his brother Kęstutis. A neat division of their dominions is illustrated by the f
2 CONC act, that Algirdas appears almost only in East Slavic sources, whereas the Western chronicles are aware of mostly Kęstutis. The Teutonic Knights in the north and the Tatar hordes in the south were equally bent on the subjection of
2 CONC  Lithuania, while Algirdas' eastern and western neighbors Muscovy and Poland generally were hostile competitors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Algirdas not only succeeded in holding his own, but acquired influence and territory at the expense of Muscovy and the Golden Horde, and extended the borders of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania to the northern shore of the Black Sea. 
2 CONC The principal efforts of Algirdas were directed at securing the Slavonic lands which had been a part of the former Kievan Rus. He procured the election of his son Andrew as the Prince of Pskov, and a powerful minority of the citiz
2 CONC ens of the Republic of Novgorod held the balance in his favor against the Muscovite influence, however, his ascendancy in both these commercial centers was at the best precarious.
2 CONT
2 CONT Algirdas occupied the important principalities of Smolensk and Bryansk in western Russia. Although his relations with the grand dukes of Muscovy were friendly on the whole, as he has married two Orthodox Russian princesses, this d
2 CONC id not prevent him from besieging Moscow in 1368 and again in 1372, both times unsuccessfully.
2 CONT
2 CONT An important feat of Algirdas was his victory over the Tatars in the Battle of Blue Waters at the Southern Bug in 1362. It resulted in the breaking up of the powerful Kipchak horde and compelled the khan to migrate still farther s
2 CONC outh and establish his headquarters for the future in Crimea.
2 CONT
2 CONT Modern historians argue, that "For Gediminas and Algirdas, retention of paganism provided a useful diplomatic tool and weapon... that allowed them to use promises of conversion as a means of preserving their power and independence
2 CONC ". According to Hermann von Wartberge and Jan Długosz, Algirdas remained a pagan until his death in the summer of 1377. Contemporary Byzantine accounts also support the Western sources: Patriarch Neilos described Algirdas as a fir
2 CONC e-worshipping prince; another Patriarch Philotheos excommunicated all Ruthenian noblemen, who helped impious Algirdas. Algirdas' pagan faith is also recorded in the 14th Byzantine historian's Nicephorus Gregoras' accounts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Algirdas' body was burned on a ceremonial pyre together with 18 horses and many of his possessions in a wood near Maišiagala' most probably in the Kukaveitis forest shrine. His descendants include the noble families of Troubetzky,
2 CONC  Czartoryski, and Sanguszko.
2 CONT
2 CONT In retrospect, Algirdas appeared to the Orthodox faithful of Ukraine and Belarus as a champion of Orthodoxy. The 16th-century Bychowiec Chronicle and 17th-century Hustynska Chronicle maintain that he converted to Orthodox Christia
2 CONC nity at some point prior to his marriage to Maria of Vitebsk in 1318. Although several Orthodox churches were indeed built in Vilnius during his reign, later assertions about his baptism find no corroboration in sources dating fro
2 CONC m Algirdas' life, leading most scholars to reject them as spurious. Despite the contemporary accounts, as well as modern studies, some Russian historians, such as Batiushikov, claim that Algirdas had been an Orthodox ruler.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, the dubious tradition about Algirdas' Orthodox conversion lived on. The commemoration book of the Kyiv Monastery of the Caves, underwritten by Algirdas' descendants, recorded his baptismal name as "Demetrius" as earl
2 CONC y as the 1460s. Following Wojciech Wijuk Kojałowicz and Macarius I, Volodymyr Antonovych writes that Algirdas took monastic vows several days before his death and was interred at the Cathedral of the Theotokos, Vilnius under the m
2 CONC onastic name Alexius
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''GDL Algirdas had 12 sons'''
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000184579624824&size=large}}
2 CONT From left counterclockwise, original name in Latin and added Lithuanian plus Christian baptismal names:
2 CONT * Sckirgollo = Skirgaila - Jan Casimir
2 CONT * Svittrigollo = Švitrigaila - Boleslaus
2 CONT * Borissus = Borisas  (has only a Christian name)
2 CONT * Coributus = Kaributas - Dymitr
2 CONT * Viguntus =Vygantas - Aleksander or Andrey (?)
2 CONT * Narimundus = Narimantas
2 CONT * Jagollo = Jogaila - Vladislaus
2 CONT * Corigollo = Karigaila - Vasili Casimir
2 CONT * Langvinus = Lengvenis - Simeon
2 CONT * Lubartus = Liubartas
2 CONT * Andreas = Andrius (has only a Christian name)
2 CONT * Buckanus = Butskus / Buckus
2 CONT
2 CONT This document is in the library of the Vatican - Biblioteca Apostolica Vaticana. The image excerpt is attached to the "Media" files within this profile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alleged sons, not mentioned in the oldest sources, but mentioned in later centuries:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Demetrius I Starszy - alleged, most likely he was a son of Vasili of Drutsk
2 CONT * Konstantin - probably the Christian name of one below listed sons with a pagan name
2 CONT * Vladimir -  probably the Christian name of one below listed sons with a pagan name
2 CONT * Fiodor -  probably the Christian name of one below listed sons with a pagan name
2 CONT
2 CONT three of these four alleged sons might be Christian names for Butskus, Narimantas, and Lubartus, as they in genealogy are only known by their pagan names.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Teodora / Fiedora 
2 CONT * NN Nowosielska
2 CONT * Agrypina 
2 CONT * Agraphena 
2 CONT * Kenna-Joanna
2 CONT * Helena of Moscow
2 CONT * Maria
2 CONT * Vilgeida - Katherine
2 CONT * Aleksandra
2 CONT * Jadwiga Oświęcimska
2 CONT * Eufrosina
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
