1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Kenna of Lithuania (baptized Joan; c. 1350 – April 27, 1368) was a 14th-century Lithuanian noblewoman and a member of the Gediminid dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Family:''' She was a daughter of Algirdas, Grand Duke of Lithuania, and his second wife, Uliana of Tver. Through her father, she was the half-sister or sister of several prominent figures in Eastern European history, includin
2 CONC g Władysław II Jagiełło, King of Poland.
2 CONT * '''Marriage:''' Around 1360, she married Casimir IV, Duke of Pomerania (also known as Casimir of Słupsk).
2 CONT * '''Dynastic Importance:''' Her marriage was a strategic political alliance arranged to forge closer ties between the Grand Duchy of Lithuania and the Pomeranian branch of the Piast dynasty. After converting to Western Christiani
2 CONC ty/Catholicism for the marriage, she was given the baptismal name Joan (Joanna).
2 CONT * '''Death:''' She died at a young age on April 27, 1368, leaving behind no known surviving children. After her death, her husband remarried. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kenna of Lithuania appears in very few primary sources, as she died at a young age (c. 18) before taking an active role in state politics. Most of what historians know about her comes from late medieval Polish chronicles and Pomer
2 CONC anian dynastic records rather than Lithuanian sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is mentioned or recorded in the following historical documents:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Chronicle of Janko of Czarnków (Kronika Janka z Czarnkowa):''' Written in the late 14th century by the Polish archdeacon Janko of Czarnków, this contemporary chronicle records Polish internal politics and dynastic alliances. 
2 CONC It details the dynastic negotiations arranged by King Casimir III the Great, including the marriage of his grandson, Casimir IV of Pomerania, to Kenna (noting her Christian baptismal name, Joan/Joanna).
2 CONT * '''Annals or Chronicles of Jan Długosz (Annales seu cronicae incliti Regni Poloniae'''): Compiled in the 15th century by the Polish historian Jan Długosz, this extensive work serves as one of the main sources for the genealogy o
2 CONC f the Gediminid dynasty and records Kenna's lineage as the daughter of Grand Duke Algirdas and Uliana of Tver.
2 CONT * '''Pomeranian Monastic Records and Necrologies''': Her death date (April 27, 1368) is preserved in regional Pomeranian church registers and obituaries (necrologia), which recorded the deaths of members of the ruling House of Gri
2 CONC ffin and their spouses for annual memorial prayers.
2 CONT * '''15th–16th Century Genealogical Tables (Genealogia):''' Works compiling the lineage of the Piast and Griffin dynasties list her under her baptismal name Johanna or Kenna as the first wife of Casimir IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Excerpt from Medilands, [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CONTENTS.htm Medieval Lands project], a major online genealogical and historical database hosted by the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy:
2 CONT
2 CONT ALGIRDAS [Olgierd], s/o GEDIMINAS Grand Duke of Lithuania & Olga Vsevolodovna of Smolensk]  ([1296]-end May 1377).  Baptized ALEKSANDER.  
2 CONT x  (1318) [MARIA] Iaroslavna of Vitebsk, d/o IAROSLAV Vasilievich, Prince of Vitebsk (-1346). 
2 CONT >1.         ANDREI 
2 CONT >2.         KONSTANTIN 
2 CONT >3.         OLEŁKO WŁODZIMIERZ 
2 CONT >4.         FEODOR 
2 CONT >5.       KORIBUTAS 
2 CONT >6.         FEODORA
2 CONT >7.         daughter 
2 CONT >8.         MARIA [Agrafena]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT xx ([1350]) IULIANA Aleksandrovna of Tver, d/o ALEXANDER I Mikhailovich Prince of Tver, ex-Grand Prince of Vladimir & Anastasia [Iurievna of Galich] ([1325]-1392). 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT >9.         '''KENNA ([1350]-27 Apr 1368).  Her marriage was proposed by her future husband's maternal grandfather, Kazimierz III King of Poland, to strengthen the ties between Lithuania and Poland[111].  She was baptised as JOHAN
2 CONC NA on her marriage.  m (1360[112]) as his first wife, KASIMIR von Pommern, son of BOGISLAW V Duke of Pomerania in Hinterpommern & his first wife Elźbieta of Poland ([1351]-2 Jan 1377).  He succeeded his father in 1373 as KASIMIR I
2 CONC V Duke of Pomerania in Stargard.  No issue.''' 
2 CONT
