1 NOTE {geni:about_me} King Władysław II Jagiełło (originally known as '''Jogaila''' in Lithuanian) lived from roughly 1351/1352 to 1434. He stands as one of the most genealogically important figures in European history. Born a pagan Gra
2 CONC nd Duke of Lithuania, he converted to Catholicism and founded the Jagiellonian dynasty, a powerful royal house that later ruled Poland, Lithuania, Bohemia, and Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==1. Parents and Dynasty Origins==
2 CONT
2 CONT Jagiełło belonged patrilineally to the Gediminid (Gediminaičiai in Lithuanian) dynasty, Lithuania's ruling family.
2 CONT * '''Father: Algirdas''', the Grand Duke of Lithuania. Through his father, Jagiełło was the grandson of '''Gediminas''', the dynasty's founder.
2 CONT * '''Mother: Uliana of Tver''', Algirdas's second wife. She was a Russian princess from the '''Rurik dynasty''' (the daughter of Alexander I, Grand Prince of Tver).
2 CONT
2 CONT ==2. Marriages and Children==
2 CONT
2 CONT Jagiełło married four times, heavily driven by his quest to secure both the Polish throne and a male heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000227673282827&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ==3. Immediate Royal Descendants==
2 CONT
2 CONT Though Jagiełło did not have surviving male heirs until very late in his life (he was roughly 70 years old when his sons were born), his lineage with Sophia of Halshany successfully secured the crown:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Władysław III of Poland (Władysław Warneńczyk): His eldest son, who became King of Poland and Hungary but died childless at the Battle of Varna. 
2 CONT * Casimir IV Jagiellon: His younger son, who inherited the crowns of Poland and Lithuania. Casimir IV married Elisabeth of Austria, and together they became known as the "Parents of Kings," extending Jagiełło's DNA into almost eve
2 CONC ry major royal household in Europe (including the Habsburgs, Tudors, and Hohenzollerns).
2 CONT
2 CONT =The primary historical documents that mention facts about his life include:=
2 CONT
2 CONT ===1. Chronicles and Contemporary Biographies===
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Annales seu cronicae incliti Regni Poloniae''' (The Annals or Chronicles of the Famous Kingdom of Poland): Written by the famous 15th-century Polish diplomat and historian Jan Długosz, this is the most exhaustive narrative so
2 CONC urce on Jagiełło's life. Długosz documented everything from the King's personal habits (like his fondness for listening to nightingales) to detailed day-by-day military accounts of the [Battle of Grunwald](https://en.wikipedia.org
2 CONC /wiki/Battle_of_Grunwald) and the political mechanics of his court.
2 CONT * '''Cronica conflictus...''' (Chronicle of the Conflict): A highly detailed contemporary account focusing exclusively on the 1410 war between the allied Polish-Lithuanian forces and the Teutonic Knights. It provides key insights 
2 CONC into Jagiełło’s battlefield command strategy.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===2. Legal Acts and Treaties===
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''The Union of Krewo (1385):''' The foundational document that altered European history. It outlines the terms under which Jagiełło agreed to convert to Catholicism, marry Queen Jadwiga, and unite the Grand Duchy of Lithuania w
2 CONC ith the Kingdom of Poland.
2 CONT * '''The Union of Horodło (1413)''': A document providing massive genealogical utility, as it granted Polish heraldic coats of arms to Lithuanian noble boyars, anchoring the family ties and lineages between the two states.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===3. Genealogical Records and Dynastic Trees===
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Genealogia Jagiellonów''' (Genealogy of the Jagiellons): Modern comprehensive tracking by historians like Zygmunt Wdowiszewski builds upon royal chancellery acts to verify his marriages—including his final union to Sophia of 
2 CONC Halshany - confirming the birth lineages of his sons Władysław III and Casimir IV. 
2 CONT * '''Ruthenian and Lithuanian Chronicles''': Sources such as the Chronicle of the Grand Dukes of Lithuania track his ancestral lineage through the Gediminid dynasty, detailing the complex power struggles he engaged in alongside hi
2 CONC s father Algirdas and his cousin Vytautas the Great. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==External Links & Sources:==
2 CONT
