1 OCCU Князь Но́вгород-Се́верский / Prince of Novgorod-Seversky / Naugardo Seversko kunigaikštis / Ruler of the Principality of Novgorod-Seversk 1362–1392, Князь новгород-северский до 1393 г.
1 TITL duke
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/LITHUANIA.htm#KorybutNovgorodSeverskiydiedafter1404
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaributas Kaributas] (Koribut, Korybut, baptized Dmitry; b. between 1350 and 1353 – d. after 1404) was a son of Algirdas, Grand Duke of Lithuania, and reigned in Severian-Novgorod until 1393.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaributas was born sometime after 1350 (the exact date is unknown) to '''Algirdas of Lithuania and Uliana of Tver'''. Born a pagan, around 1380 he was baptized in the Orthodox rite and became the prince of Severian Novgorod. He ad
2 CONC opted the Christian name of Dmitry and hence is sometimes referred to as Dmitry Korybut (a combination of his Slavicised Lithuanian name Kaributas and his Christian name). Kaributas appeared in politics during the Lithuanian Civil
2 CONC  War (1381–1384) when he supported his brother Jogaila against his uncle Kęstutis and cousin Vytautas. In 1382, he began a rebellion in Severian Novgorod, engaging Kęstutis' forces so that Jogaila could attack and capture lightly 
2 CONC guarded Vilnius, the capital of the Grand Duchy. Kaributas also witnessed the Treaty of Dubysa with the Teutonic Knights.
2 CONT
2 CONT For his service, Kaributas was awarded possessions in Navahrudak and Lida. Kaributas continued to support Jogaila: he witnessed the Union of Krewo and fought in the Lithuanian Civil War (1389–1392). After the Ostrów Agreement, Kar
2 CONC ibutas refused to recognize Vytautas' superiority and was defeated in a battle near Lida in early 1393. Kaributas was imprisoned and stripped of his possessions. However, he was soon released and given Zbarazh, Braslav, and Vinnyt
2 CONC sa. Severian Novgorod was given to Fedor, son of Liubartas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaributas last appears in written sources in 1404, during a military campaign waged by Vytautas against the Principality of Smolensk.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaributas' male-line descendants included Princes Zbaraski, Wiśniowiecki, Woroniecki, and Nieswicki. Polish king Michael Korybut Wiśniowiecki was named Korybut to foreground his agnatic descent from Kaributas.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage and issue'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaributas married Princess Anastasia, daughter of Grand Prince Oleg II of Ryazan, with whom he had three daughters and three sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Helena (wife of John II "the Iron" Duke of Racibórz),
2 CONT * Fedor of Nesvich, Volhynia
2 CONT * Sigismund Korybut (a claimant to the Bohemian Crown),
2 CONT * Nastasia (wife of Fedor of Kashin)
2 CONT * Ivan
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Information from the genealogy of the Golitzyn Family: Korybut of Novgorod - Severski died after 1404.
2 CONT
2 CONT Korybut Dymitr (z drugiego; † po 1404); ks. nowogrodzko-siewierski 1386-1392, chrzest w obrządku wschodnim 1386; × Anastazja; (1°-v. Dymitr, ks. drucki)
2 CONT
2 CONT D1. Iwan (†po 1431); × NN; (2°-v. Borys ks. Gliński)
2 CONT D2. Zygmunt (†1435); pretendent do tronu czeskiego 1422-1427
2 CONT D3. Fiodor; 1422-1440; może identyczny z ks. Fedkiem Nieświzkim, przodkiem KS. ZBARASKICH i KS. WIŚNIOWIECKICH
2 CONT D4. Maria; × Fiodor ks. Worotyński
2 CONT D5. Anastazja; × [p. 1400] Wasyl, ks. kaszyński (†1426)
2 CONT D6. Helena (†po 2.III.1449); × [p. 16.I.1407] Jan II, ks. raciborski (†1424 (12.VIII?))
