1 NAME Малгожата дочь Петра /Кмита/
2 GIVN Малгожата дочь Петра
2 SURN Кмита
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1407
2 PLAC Królestwo Polskie 
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} notę by Natalia Gurko:
2 CONT
2 CONT =Małgorzata Kmita of the Szreniawa coat of arms
2 CONT
2 CONT was the daughter of Piotr Lunak Kmita, Cup-bearer of Sandomierz, and Katarzyna Rzeszowska. Her full brother was Jan Kmita, called Tępy, who died in 1434 and was married to Barbara Wątróbczanka of Strzelce.
2 CONT
2 CONT Małgorzata was married twice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her first husband was Jan Goły, also recorded in the sources as Gołyjan, Golian or Goligyan, of Strzałkowo and Obichów, who died in 1437. They had three children: Stanisław, Anna and Małgorzata.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1445 Małgorzata transferred one third of her hereditary estates to her children, including half of Lipnica Murowana, while retaining the right to use these estates for another seven years. The document names all three children:
2 CONC  Anna, Małgorzata and Stanisław.
2 CONT
2 CONT Małgorzata’s second husband was Przedpełk Mościc of Wielki Koźmin, who died in 1448. There were no children from this marriage. Małgorzata is already recorded as Przedpełk’s wife in 1441, during the division of property with Mikoł
2 CONC aj Kmita, Castellan of Przemyśl.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her brother Jan Kmita and his children, Małgorzata acquired inheritance rights to a substantial part of the estates belonging to this branch of the Kmita family.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1436 her first husband, Jan Golian, represented her in a dispute with Mikołaj Kmita, who had taken possession of estates claimed by Małgorzata by right of proximity after the death of her full brother Jan and his children. The 
2 CONC dispute concerned, among other properties, Bachórz, Denowiec, Dynów and numerous villages belonging to the Dynów and Bachórz estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a result of the division made in 1441, Małgorzata received, among other properties, Bachórz, Denowiec, the town of Dynów with its suburb, and numerous surrounding villages. Mikołaj Kmita received Sobień Castle with its dependen
2 CONC t villages, Bachórzec and Dubiecko.
2 CONT
2 CONT In later years Małgorzata appears in the sources as the owner of Dynów and the Dynów estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is still recorded in 1462, when, as Małgorzata of Dynów, she made a grant to the Church of the Blessed Virgin Mary in Dynów.
2 CONT
2 CONT Małgorzata’s descendants from her first marriage were closely associated with the Dynów estates. Her son Stanisław later appears as Stanisław Denowski. The name Denowski was derived from Dynów and became the name used by this bran
2 CONC ch of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stanisław is sometimes referred to in historical literature as Mościc Denowski, but this does not mean that Przedpełk Mościc was his father. His father was Jan Goły, or Golian of Obichów, who belonged to the Wieniawa coat of arms.
2 CONC  Boniecki points out that Długosz describes Stanisław’s son, Jan Denowski, as a Wieniawita, a member of the Wieniawa clan. This confirms that this line belonged to the coat of arms of Jan Denowski’s father and grandfather.
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughter Anna appears in later sources as Anna of Rzeszów (Rzeszowska).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1470 she is recorded as the wife of Jan Sapieński of Sepno and appears in property matters concerning Stanisław Denowski.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second daughter, Małgorzata, is directly recorded as a daughter of Małgorzata Kmita in a document from 1445. Her later fate has not yet been established.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Słownik historyczno-geograficzny ziem polskich w średniowieczu; Adam Boniecki, Herbarz Polski.
