1 NAME Анна, дочь Яна Голиана из Обихова, по прозвищу Жешовская
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1428
2 PLAC Królestwo Polskie 
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1481
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Pyzdry II - 1369, 1359
2 CONT
2 CONT notę by Natalia Gurko:
2 CONT
2 CONT =Anna of Rzeszów (Rzeszowska), Wieniawa coat of arms
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna was the daughter of Jan Goły, also recorded in the sources as Gołyjan, Golian or Goligyan, of Strzałków and Obichów, and Małgorzata Kmita of the Szreniawa coat of arms, daughter of Piotr Lunak Kmita and Katarzyna Rzeszowska. 
2 CONC Anna had a brother, Stanisław, later known as Stanisław Denowski, and a sister, Małgorzata.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through her father, Anna belonged to the family of the Wieniawa coat of arms. Her connection with Rzeszów came through her mother’s family and through the estates she inherited from her. For this reason, the name Anna z Rzeszowa f
2 CONC ound in the sources, and the later form Rzeszowska, reflects her connection with Rzeszów and her property there rather than descent in the male line from the Rzeszowski family.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1445, Anna, together with her sister Małgorzata and brother Stanisław, received one third of their mother’s hereditary estates, including half of Lipnica Murowana. Their mother was to keep these estates for another seven years 
2 CONC and then transfer them to her children. The document names all three children: Anna, Małgorzata and Stanisław.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna was married twice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her first husband was Filip Furman of Żerków and Niezamyśl, castellan of Międzyrzecz. This marriage is confirmed by property records. In 1462, Małgorzata of Dynów transferred the village of Błażowa, valued at 1,000 grzywnas, to Fi
2 CONC lip of Niezamyśl as the dowry of her daughter Anna.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna and Filip had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Jan of Żerków, later of Dynów.
2 CONT 2. Mikołaj of Żerków and Rzeszów.
2 CONT 3. Piotr of Żerków and Rzeszów.
2 CONT 4. Małgorzata of Żerków and Dynów, wife of Maciej of Niebieszczany. In a document from 1496, Mikołaj and Piotr of Rzeszów are named as her brothers.
2 CONT 5. Jadwiga, later wife of Jan Wojszyk of Żmigród.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Filip’s death, Anna appears in the sources as his widow. In 1466 she is recorded as Anna Denowska, widow of Filip, confirming both her connection with Dynów and her marriage to Filip.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna’s second husband was Jan Sapieński of Sepno. No children from this marriage have been identified in the sources used here.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1470, Anna of Rzeszów is recorded as the wife of Jan Sapieński. In the same year, her brother Stanisław Denowski made commitments to her concerning property rights and family estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1475, Jan of Dynów undertook to release Anna’s share of Rzeszów and the surrounding estates and to give Anna, then described as the wife of Jan Sapieński, possession of Dynów and several villages.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1480-1481, Anna continued to pursue her inheritance rights in court. She brought claims against Jan of Dynów for failing to fulfil earlier obligations concerning her shares of Rzeszów and her rights to Dynów, Harta, Bachórz and
2 CONC  other estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Property records also provide information about Anna’s children from her first marriage. In 1488, Anna granted her daughter Jadwiga 1,000 grzywnas secured on halves of Błażowa, Barycz, Kąkolówka, Futoma and Piątkowa. Jadwiga later
2 CONC  became the wife of Jan Wojszyk of Żmigród. Anna’s sons Mikołaj and Piotr of Rzeszów and her daughter Małgorzata, wife of Maciej of Niebieszczany, also appear in records from the late fifteenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT The surviving documents therefore allow Anna’s life to be traced over several decades, from her appearance among the children of Małgorzata Kmita in 1445 to property records from the 1480s. Her marriages and the later history of h
2 CONC er children show the continuation of this branch of the family through estates in Rzeszów, Dynów and the surrounding area.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna appears in the sources under several territorial names, especially Anna z Rzeszowa and Anna Denowska. These names refer to the same woman and reflect her connections with different family estates. The genealogical name Rzeszo
2 CONC wska should therefore be understood primarily as a territorial designation rather than the surname of her paternal family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Słownik historyczno-geograficzny ziem polskich w średniowieczu; Adam Boniecki, Herbarz Polski.
