1 OCCU наместник оршанский, оболецкий, витебский;староста, наместник, воевода смоленский, староста мерецкий и волковысский 1518-1520
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} note by Natalia Gurko:
2 CONT
2 CONT =Yuri Hlebowicz, of the Leliwa coat of arms,
2 CONT
2 CONT son of Hleb Wiażewicz, was one of the prominent officials of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania in the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries whose career was closely connected with the lands of Smolensk and Vitebsk.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1490 to 1499 he served as governor of Smolensk. In 1500 he is recorded as governor of Orsha and Oboltsy, and from 1503 he was governor of Vitebsk. When the Smolensk Voivodeship was established in 1508, he became its first voi
2 CONC vode and held the office until spring 1514.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Smolensk was lost under Voivode Yuri Sologub, Yuri Hlebowicz continued his service in another part of the Grand Duchy. From 1514 to 1518 he held Merecz, and subsequently received Wołkowysk together with the districts of Wołk
2 CONC owysk, Porozów and Nowy Dwór in compensation for the loss of the Merecz starosty. A privilege dated 18 November 1518 granted him Wołkowysk for life. Yuri Hlebowicz is still mentioned in documents in July 1521.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources from the early sixteenth century also make it possible to identify Mikołaj Yurievich Hlebowicz, the future starosta of Drohiczyn, as his son. In documents of 1509-1511 he is repeatedly referred to as “Mikołaj, Yuri’s son, 
2 CONC Hlebowicz.” Mikołaj was detained in the Muscovite state during negotiations concerning the release and mutual return of persons held by the two sides. The negotiations involved Stanisław Hlebowicz, Voivode of Polotsk, Yuri’s broth
2 CONC er and thus Mikołaj’s uncle. Mikołaj died before his father, sometime before January 1514.
2 CONT
2 CONT The principal details are confirmed by the published lists of officeholders for the Smolensk Land, Podlasie and the Nowogródek Voivodeship, as well as by published records of the Lithuanian Metrica.
