1 OCCU literat / польский писатель и музыкант, политик и общественный деятель эпохи Возрождения, шляхтич герба Окша.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The 16th century Polish Nobility: http://www.almanachdegotha.org/id221.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Mikołaj Rej or Mikołaj Rey of Nagłowice (February 4, 1505 – between September 8 and October 5, 1569) was a Polish poet and prose writer of the emerging Renaissance in Poland as it succeeded the Middle Ages, as well as a politician
2 CONC  and musician. He was the first Polish author to write exclusively in the Polish language, and is considered (with Biernat of Lublin and Jan Kochanowski), to be one of the founders of Polish literary language and literature.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rej was born into a noble family at Żurawno, near Halicz. His father had moved to Ruthenia from Nagłowice, near Kraków, Poland at the invitation of archbishop Jan Wątróbka. He married Barbara née Herburt there. She was the mother 
2 CONC of Mikołaj. Although young Rej received little formal education in Lwów, and, at the age of 13 attended but one year at the Kraków Academy, he managed to educate himself by studying Latin literature.
2 CONT
2 CONT Approximately 1524, Rej began his service at the court of voivode Andrzej Tęczyński in Sandomierz. There, he acquired most of his vast knowledge in the field of humanities. He returned to his family’s town of Topola and married Zo
2 CONC fia Kosnówna (Kościeniówna), a relative of the archbishop of Gniezno.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1531 Rej moved to Kobyle, in the Chełm area, which had been bequeathed to his wife, and thereafter, he frequented the court of Hetman Mikołaj Sieniawski. In 1541 or 1548 Rej converted to Calvinism. He took part in synods and fo
2 CONC unded Protestant schools and communities on his lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rej took part in sejms and thought his writing an important social mission. He was the first Polish writer to receive a substantial reward for his output. By the end of his life, he owned several villages and oversaw many. He rece
2 CONC ived Temerowce from King Zygmunt I the Old, and Dziewięciele from King Zygmunt II August as a lifelong possession and two towns, one of them Rejowiec, founded by Rej in 1547. Living during the Golden Liberty embraced by the Polish
2 CONC  nobility, tolerance characterized his oversight and this philosophy was carried on by his sons. Rej died at Rejowiec in 1569.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the five-hundredth anniversary of his birth, Mikolaj Rej was described as a "father of Polish literature", and it also was noted that his grandson, Andrzej Rej, royal secretary and Calvinist, is Mikolaj's most prominent offspri
2 CONC ng. That grandson may be the subject of the 1637 painting by Rembrandt, A Polish Nobleman (perhaps, painted while he was visiting Amsterdam during a trip as a Polish ambassador on a diplomatic mission to the courts of the Danish, 
2 CONC the English, and the Dutch).
